 write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of induction, ${a}_{n}={4}^{n}+{3}^{n}$ is true for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418681 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418682 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418687 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418713 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418715 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418722 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418730 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418726 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418730 1069 "q-183891" 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418730 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418730 1071 "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=9{a}_{n}-8{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=59,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to prove the following statement using strong induction:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5,\\,∀n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base cases $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={{field-1}} \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ is true for all {{field-3}} for {{field-4}}. In particular, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k}={{field-5}} \\\\ {a}_{k-1}={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418730 1072 #uuid "90b99752-34a5-5bfa-9d99-9e9bd7bb0b61" 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418730 1074 "To prove a statement using strong induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula ${a}_{n+1}=9{a}_{n}-8{a}_{n-1}$ does not apply until ${a}_{3},$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1$ and $n=2.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={8}^{1}-5 \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={8}^{2}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base cases are true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the right-hand side and show that it equals the left-hand side in both cases.\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\le k,$ where $k\\ge 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ is true for all $n\\le k$ for $k\\ge 2.$ In particular, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={8}^{k}-5 \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={8}^{k-1}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={8}^{k+1}-5.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418676 13194139534320 17592186045417 true] [17592186418731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186418679 13194139534320 17592